/**
 * @mainpage Sushi Slash
 *
 * @section overview Overview
 *
 * Sushi Slash is an LCOM action arcade game inspired by Fruit Ninja. Sushi
 * pieces are launched across the screen and the player cuts them with fast
 * mouse slashes before they fall away. The game tracks score, lives, elapsed
 * session time, current real-world time, and progressively increases pressure
 * through wave and difficulty scheduling.
 *
 * @section gameplay Core Gameplay
 *
 * - Regular sushi awards score when sliced and costs a life when missed.
 * - Bombs are hazards: slicing one ends the current run.
 * - Cut sushi pieces split into independent fragments with their own physics.
 * - Waves control when sushi and hazards appear.
 * - Difficulty scales from EASY to HARD as the player score increases.
 *
 * @section devices Device Responsibilities
 *
 * - Graphics Card: renders the background, sprites, fragments, menus, HUD, and
 *   mouse slash effects.
 * - Mouse: controls the cursor and creates slash gestures used for hit
 *   detection.
 * - Keyboard: handles menu navigation and future Boss Sushi key sequences.
 * - Timer: drives the game loop, physics updates, animations, waves, and
 *   session timing.
 * - RTC: provides the real-world clock shown in the UI.
 * - Serial Port: reserved for future multiplayer score or input exchange.
 *
 * @section architecture Project Structure
 *
 * The public API is organized under @c proj/include:
 *
 * - @c game/: gameplay state, sushi objects, waves, menus, boss/combo data,
 *   sprites, and queues.
 * - @c ui/: drawing helpers, screen-specific menu drawing, HUD/status bar,
 *   mouse trail rendering, and the session timer.
 * - @c macros/: shared constants for screen geometry, physics, menus, and
 *   mouse slash tuning.
 * - @c font.h and @c init.h: bitmap font and shared asset initialization APIs.
 *
 * @section flow Runtime Flow
 *
 * A normal run starts in the main menu. When singleplayer begins,
 * gameplay_start() initializes the session data and wave runtime. Each timer
 * tick calls gameplay_update() to advance waves, item physics, missed-item
 * handling, fragments, and difficulty. Mouse input is processed through
 * gameplay_handle_cut(), then gameplay_draw() and the UI helpers render the
 * current frame.
 *
 * @section docs Documentation Guide
 *
 * Start with game.h for the main state machine and gameplay lifecycle, sushi.h
 * for flying objects and cut fragments, waves.h for spawn scheduling, and ui.h
 * for rendering helpers. Menu-specific state and drawing APIs live in
 * menu_state.h, pause_menu_state.h, game_over.h, menu_draw.h,
 * pause_menu_draw.h, and game_over_draw.h.
 */
